{
  "dom_id": "step-q94282",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation.",
  "worked_solution": "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4R\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=4R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$",
